From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Sun, 01 Nov 2026 12:30:39 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7996: size the WED RX buffer pool for one
 PCIe layout

The WED RX pool takes 32768 buffers on a board with a second PCIe
function and 24576 on a board without one. That is 64 MiB against
48 MiB of page memory, plus a token object per buffer. The WED posts
three RX rings from the pool, and the reorder windows hold the rest.
The demand therefore follows the stations and the loss on the air,
not the PCIe layout.

One size of 24576 serves both layouts. A unit with the second PCIe
function keeps its free count far above the posted rings under a
saturating load.

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
 mt7996/mmio.c |    3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/mt7996/mmio.c
+++ b/mt7996/mmio.c
@@ -17,6 +17,8 @@
 static bool wed_enable;
 module_param(wed_enable, bool, 0644);
 
+#define MT7996_WED_RX_NPKT	24576
+
 #define INVALID_REG_ADDR	0xffffffff
 
 static const struct __base mt7996_reg_base[] = {
@@ -567,7 +569,7 @@ int mt7996_mmio_wed_init(struct mt7996_d
 					MT7996_RXQ_MSDU_PG_BAND0 * MT_RING_SIZE;
 
 		wed->wlan.rx_nbuf = 65536;
-		wed->wlan.rx_npkt = dev->hif2 ? 32768 : 24576;
+		wed->wlan.rx_npkt = MT7996_WED_RX_NPKT;
 		wed->wlan.rx_size = SKB_WITH_OVERHEAD(MT_RX_BUF_SIZE);
 
 		wed->wlan.rx_tbit[0] = ffs(MT_INT_RX_DONE_BAND0) - 1;
